WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.909 --> 00:00:04.130
Audiences aren't the only ones who get the
willies from horror movies.

00:00:04.130 --> 00:00:08.580
For some actors, the process of starring in
a film full of jump scares and weapon-wielding

00:00:08.580 --> 00:00:12.120
maniacs can leave some long-lasting emotional
marks.

00:00:12.120 --> 00:00:16.400
In the case of these scary movies' stars,
being a part of a fright flick took a toll

00:00:16.400 --> 00:00:21.099
on their psyches during, and sometimes even
after, production.

00:00:21.099 --> 00:00:22.230
The chestburster

00:00:22.230 --> 00:00:27.440
The chest-bursting scene from Alien ranks
among the most iconic moments in all of sci-fi

00:00:27.440 --> 00:00:28.740
film history.

00:00:28.740 --> 00:00:32.090
And there's no doubt that it works so well
because the cast really didn't know what was

00:00:32.090 --> 00:00:36.090
about to happen and were genuinely shocked
and terrified by it.

00:00:36.090 --> 00:00:40.370
Director Ridley Scott explained his reason
for letting the creature's cruel reveal happen

00:00:40.370 --> 00:00:43.480
in real-time during the shoot, by telling
Empire Magazine:

00:00:43.480 --> 00:00:46.720
"The reactions were going to be the most difficult
thing.

00:00:46.720 --> 00:00:51.989
If an actor is just acting terrified, you
can't get the genuine look of raw, animal

00:00:51.989 --> 00:00:53.149
fear."

00:00:53.149 --> 00:00:56.149
His approach to eliciting sincere scares worked.

00:00:56.149 --> 00:00:58.219
As actress Veronica Cartwright later remembered:

00:00:58.219 --> 00:01:03.160
"You see this thing start to come out, so
we all get sucked in, we lean forward to check

00:01:03.160 --> 00:01:04.160
it out.

00:01:04.160 --> 00:01:05.160
[...] All of a sudden it comes out.

00:01:05.160 --> 00:01:07.500
I tell you, none of us expected it.

00:01:07.500 --> 00:01:09.400
It came out and twisted round."

00:01:09.400 --> 00:01:14.040
Cartwright reportedly passed out as a result
of the scene and still talks about how upset

00:01:14.040 --> 00:01:16.190
it made her to this day.

00:01:16.190 --> 00:01:17.400
The claws

00:01:17.400 --> 00:01:21.790
The real-life ghost-hunting history of Ed
and Lorraine Warren is freaky enough to put

00:01:21.790 --> 00:01:23.080
anyone on edge.

00:01:23.080 --> 00:01:27.890
But actress Vera Farmiga knew very little
of her character's real-life counterpart before

00:01:27.890 --> 00:01:32.740
signing on for The Conjuring, and receiving
her first paranormal encounter as a welcome

00:01:32.740 --> 00:01:33.740
gift.

00:01:33.740 --> 00:01:37.390
While she was doing research on Warren's work,
she had a brush with the beyond after she

00:01:37.390 --> 00:01:40.250
hung up the phone with director James Wan.

00:01:40.250 --> 00:01:41.670
CinemaBlend reported her saying,

00:01:41.670 --> 00:01:46.610
"I opened the computer screen and there were
three digital claw marks, from the upper right

00:01:46.610 --> 00:01:48.850
diagonal to the lower left."

00:01:48.850 --> 00:01:53.280
It wasn't enough to steer her away from the
movie, but the haunting experience did follow

00:01:53.280 --> 00:01:54.810
her through to the film's end.

00:01:54.810 --> 00:01:58.470
The day after she finished shooting the first
film and returned to her home in New York,

00:01:58.470 --> 00:02:03.820
she woke up to find something even scarier:
three claw-mark bruises on her thigh.

00:02:03.820 --> 00:02:07.930
Even though she chose not to "give into the
fear" of the moment, she said she had to build

00:02:07.930 --> 00:02:12.160
up an "emotional armor" to deal with what
she experienced with those series of scratch

00:02:12.160 --> 00:02:13.880
marks.

00:02:13.880 --> 00:02:15.090
The curtain

00:02:15.090 --> 00:02:19.230
When it comes to the horror genre, Patrick
Wilson is something of a scream king.

00:02:19.230 --> 00:02:23.610
The actor's filmography is filled with freaky
fare, but it was the second Conjuring film

00:02:23.610 --> 00:02:25.760
that freaked the actor out the most.

00:02:25.760 --> 00:02:30.420
According to him, there was a "pretty trippy"
moment during production involving a curtain

00:02:30.420 --> 00:02:33.850
haunting that led the crew to bring in a priest
to bless the set.

00:02:33.850 --> 00:02:34.850
He told Metro:

00:02:34.850 --> 00:02:39.410
"It was a huge curtain that went from the
floor to the ceiling, which was sort of waving

00:02:39.410 --> 00:02:43.810
violently and there was no door open or fan
on, no nothing.

00:02:43.810 --> 00:02:48.760
[...] You didn't even hear any air, but you
watched these curtains sort of violently going."

00:02:48.760 --> 00:02:53.010
Wilson considers himself a skeptic, but this
moment may have gotten the better of him because

00:02:53.010 --> 00:02:56.600
he's admitted to believing his own house may
be haunted now.

00:02:56.600 --> 00:02:57.720
He told The Independent:

00:02:57.720 --> 00:03:02.680
"I've heard people on two different occasions
say they've heard kids' laughter in the middle

00:03:02.680 --> 00:03:04.030
of the night in my house."

00:03:04.030 --> 00:03:08.040
Perhaps he should do a few more comedies in
between haunted house pics.

00:03:08.040 --> 00:03:09.150
The visitor

00:03:09.150 --> 00:03:15.500
James Brolin was initially unsure about the
1979 adaptation of Jay Anson's true story-inspired

00:03:15.500 --> 00:03:17.420
novel The Amityville Horror.

00:03:18.160 --> 00:03:20.600
"I didn't realize what I was getting into."

00:03:20.600 --> 00:03:25.319
He gave the book a look and got so frightened
by what happened to him mid-read that he accepted

00:03:25.319 --> 00:03:27.140
the gig right then and there.

00:03:27.140 --> 00:03:28.670
Brolin explained to The A.V.

00:03:28.670 --> 00:03:29.670
Club:

00:03:29.670 --> 00:03:33.240
"I would hang my pants on the door of the
bedroom [...] and all of a sudden the pants

00:03:33.240 --> 00:03:35.180
fell off the door and onto the floor.

00:03:35.180 --> 00:03:38.740
[...] I was at a scary part of this book,
and so it surprised me that I started laughing

00:03:38.740 --> 00:03:41.790
after I recovered and said, I've got to do
this movie!'"

00:03:41.790 --> 00:03:46.800
And he wasn't the only Amityville alum to
succumb to superstition associated with the

00:03:46.800 --> 00:03:47.800
series.

00:03:47.800 --> 00:03:52.870
The 2005 remake was also the site of some
freakish coincidences, such as a fisherman's

00:03:52.870 --> 00:03:56.730
body washing up on the set's shore as filming
geared up to begin.

00:03:56.730 --> 00:03:58.040
Ryan Reynolds told MovieWeb:

00:03:58.040 --> 00:04:03.500
"A lot of the crew were waking up at 3:15
in the morning which was when all these atrocities

00:04:03.500 --> 00:04:05.100
in the house took place each time.

00:04:05.100 --> 00:04:07.040
I think it was a subconscious thing.

00:04:07.040 --> 00:04:10.819
You read the script and suddenly pop awake
at 3:15 in the morning."

00:04:10.819 --> 00:04:14.520
Even if it's just a coincidence, it's still
creepy.

00:04:14.520 --> 00:04:15.520
The box

00:04:15.520 --> 00:04:20.200
He might be playing a savage, bat-slinging
brute of the zombie apocalypse in The Walking

00:04:20.200 --> 00:04:25.530
Dead, but Jeffrey Dean Morgan got pretty worked
up over his time on the set of The Possession.

00:04:25.530 --> 00:04:29.970
He admitted to being so freaked out by the
real story of the dybbuk box that they made

00:04:29.970 --> 00:04:32.660
sure not to take it lightly during filming.

00:04:32.660 --> 00:04:33.740
He told io9:

00:04:33.740 --> 00:04:36.810
"There was some weird goings on on set.

00:04:36.810 --> 00:04:38.639
Lots of light bulbs exploding.

00:04:38.639 --> 00:04:42.640
Just overall kind of creepiness […] 'Don't
mock the box' was sort of the mantra that

00:04:42.640 --> 00:04:44.790
we lived by while we were filming this."

00:04:44.790 --> 00:04:49.600
He purposefully steered clear of the real
box that inspired the film because, saying:

00:04:49.600 --> 00:04:53.200
"Somebody wanted to bring the box to the set,
the real box.

00:04:53.200 --> 00:04:55.060
I was adamantly against it.

00:04:55.060 --> 00:04:59.810
If you go and you look at what has happened
to people that have had contact with an actual

00:04:59.810 --> 00:05:02.530
dybbuk box, it's not good stuff.

00:05:02.530 --> 00:05:03.990
[…] Why risk that?"

00:05:03.990 --> 00:05:04.990
Why indeed.

00:05:04.990 --> 00:05:06.710
The clown

00:05:06.710 --> 00:05:11.030
Capturing the essence of Pennywise the Clown
for the cinematic adaptation of Stephen King's

00:05:11.030 --> 00:05:13.270
It would be no easy feat for anyone.

00:05:13.270 --> 00:05:18.350
Not only was there a prior portrayal by Tim
Curry to live up to, but unlike most villains,

00:05:18.350 --> 00:05:22.970
there are no human or redeeming qualities
to latch onto to make an actor feel better

00:05:22.970 --> 00:05:24.340
about playing him.

00:05:24.340 --> 00:05:28.430
Understandably, for Bill Skarsgård Skarsgard,
the process of suiting up as the child-slayer

00:05:28.430 --> 00:05:29.770
was no picnic.

00:05:29.770 --> 00:05:34.480
The actor has said that the process of spending
time with this character was like a "destructive

00:05:34.480 --> 00:05:37.930
relationship" that he was relieved to end
when his part wrapped.

00:05:37.930 --> 00:05:42.389
Even putting away Pennywise's costume wasn't
enough to keep the bad vibes at bay, though.

00:05:42.389 --> 00:05:43.700
He told Entertainment Weekly:

00:05:43.700 --> 00:05:49.830
"I was home, done with the movie, and I started
having very strange and vivid Pennywise dreams

00:05:49.830 --> 00:05:52.190
[...] Every night, he came and visited.

00:05:52.190 --> 00:05:57.270
It was in the shape of either me dealing with
him, sort of Pennywise as a separate entity

00:05:57.270 --> 00:06:02.150
of me, and then also me as Pennywise in circumstances
that I didn't appreciate.

00:06:02.150 --> 00:06:06.260
Like, I'm Pennywise and I'm really upset that
I'm out in public and people are looking at

00:06:06.260 --> 00:06:07.260
me."

00:06:07.260 --> 00:06:12.370
Sounds awful, but it couldn't have been too
bad — Skarsgård signed on to reprise his

00:06:12.370 --> 00:06:13.920
role for It: Chapter Two.

